('wikiloc', '214041656', 'https://www.wikiloc.com/hiking-trails/el-serrallo-mirador-isabel-ii-monte-tortuga-torre-de-anyera-benzu-214041656', 'Ceuta: El Serrallo - Mirador Isabel II - Monte Tortuga - Monte Anyera - Cala Mocarro', '{"en":"One way via Monte Tortuga, Cala Mocarro","ca":"Un sol sentit via Monte Tortuga, Cala Mocarro","es":"Un solo sentido vía Monte Tortuga, Cala Mocarro"}'::jsonb, 'Ceuta: El Serrallo - Mirador Isabel II - Monte Tortuga - Monte Anyera - Cala Mocarro Hiking trail in El Serrallo, Ceuta (España). Download its GPS track and follow the itinerary on a map. During a weekend in Ceuta, we went on this wonderful hike through the green thicket of Ceuta, past some of the many watchtowers. 
 
The trail began with a rather sobering view of the heavily secured border fence with Morocco. A sight that is very reminiscent of the divided Germany. 
 
The view from the Mirador de Isabel II was more uplifting, or rather fantastic. From up here, we had a good view of the city of Ceuta with its Monte Hacho, and some of the surrounding area to the right and left. 
 
We then continued to the Fuerte Isabel II, whose construction and function were explained to us in detail by our hiking guide. 
 
The next stop, a Spanish barracks, was fondly remembered by some of our fellow hikers because they had to do their compulsory military service there. 
 
We then continued down a beautiful valley, which was covered in vegetation and wonderfully green. This section led us to a shooting range, and from there we had magnificent views of Monte de la Tortuga, on top of which were the ruins of another watchtower. From here, we were able to admire the Jabal Mousa in all its glory. 
 
A little further downhill, we made a detour to Ceuta''s highest mountain, Monte Anyera, with its watchtower, Torre Anyera. Once again, we were given detailed explanations. 
 
After retracing our steps we walked along the Senda de los Helechos, which lives up to its name, as huge ferns grew to the right and left of us. 
 
Just before the Torre de la Alquería, we were able to refresh our water supplies with delicious cold spring water. 
 
We continued down the mostly dry stream bed to Cala Mocarro beach. Here, we not only enjoyed a swim in the fresh sea water, but also had a picnic and rested for a while before being picked up a good two hours later and taken back to the city. 
A beautiful hike through the surprisingly green and varied countryside around Ceuta.', '{"en":"This easy 11.6 km one-way hike through Ceuta''s green landscape offers a journey past numerous watchtowers and viewpoints. Starting near the border fence, the route quickly ascends to the Mirador de Isabel II, providing panoramic views of the city and Monte Hacho.\n\nThe trail continues to Fuerte Isabel II and passes a former barracks before descending into a lush valley. It then leads to a shooting range with views of Monte de la Tortuga and its ruined watchtower. A detour takes hikers to Monte Anyera, Ceuta''s highest point, marked by Torre Anyera, before retracing steps.","ca":"Aquesta caminada fàcil d''11,6 km d''anada per l''entorn verd de Ceuta ofereix un recorregut per nombroses torres de vigilància i miradors. Començant prop de la tanca fronterera, la ruta ascendeix ràpidament al Mirador de Isabel II, oferint vistes panoràmiques de la ciutat i el Monte Hacho.\n\nEl sender continua fins al Fuerte Isabel II i passa per una antiga caserna abans de descendir a una vall exuberant. Després condueix a un camp de tir amb vistes al Monte de la Tortuga i la seva torre de vigilància en ruïnes. Un desviament porta els excursionistes al Monte Anyera, el punt més alt de Ceuta, marcat per la Torre Anyera, abans de desfer el camí.","es":"Esta caminata fácil de 11,6 km de ida a través del paisaje verde de Ceuta ofrece un recorrido por numerosas torres de vigilancia y miradores. Comenzando cerca de la valla fronteriza, la ruta asciende rápidamente al Mirador de Isabel II, brindando vistas panorámicas de la ciudad y el Monte Hacho.\n\nEl sendero continúa hasta el Fuerte Isabel II y pasa por un antiguo cuartel antes de descender a un valle exuberante. Luego conduce a un campo de tiro con vistas al Monte de la Tortuga y su torre de vigilancia en ruinas. Un desvío lleva a los excursionistas al Monte Anyera, el punto más alto de Ceuta, marcado por la Torre Anyera, antes de desandar el ca